====================================================================================================
J1(a) PRODUCT IDENTITY WITH EXACT BINOMIALS at the floor-optimal l (producer model, integer l nearest l*)
====================================================================================================
  identity: 2^l * N/C(2^l,m) * C(2^l,m-1) = N * m/(2^l - m + 1)  (exact)  vs  m*N (approximation used by the producer)
  m=2 l=43: C(F,m-1)/C(F,m) = m/(F-m+1); relative error of m/F approx = 1.137e-13; product/(m N) - 1 = 1.137e-13; log2 product = 132.000000 vs log2(m N) = 132.000000
  m=3 l=33: C(F,m-1)/C(F,m) = m/(F-m+1); relative error of m/F approx = 2.328e-10; product/(m N) - 1 = 2.328e-10; log2 product = 132.584963 vs log2(m N) = 132.584963
  m=4 l=27: C(F,m-1)/C(F,m) = m/(F-m+1); relative error of m/F approx = 2.235e-08; product/(m N) - 1 = 2.235e-08; log2 product = 133.000000 vs log2(m N) = 133.000000
  m=5 l=23: C(F,m-1)/C(F,m) = m/(F-m+1); relative error of m/F approx = 4.768e-07; product/(m N) - 1 = 4.768e-07; log2 product = 133.321929 vs log2(m N) = 133.321928
  m=6 l=20: C(F,m-1)/C(F,m) = m/(F-m+1); relative error of m/F approx = 4.768e-06; product/(m N) - 1 = 4.768e-06; log2 product = 133.584969 vs log2(m N) = 133.584963
  m=8 l=16: C(F,m-1)/C(F,m) = m/(F-m+1); relative error of m/F approx = 1.068e-04; product/(m N) - 1 = 1.068e-04; log2 product = 134.000154 vs log2(m N) = 134.000000
  -> identity holds exactly up to the factor F/(F-m+1) = 1 + (m-1)/(F-m+1); at every optimal l this is below 1e-4 relative.

====================================================================================================
J1(b) UNIFORM-SPREAD / ONE-RELATION-PER-TARGET ASSUMPTION at l*: lambda = C(2^l,m)/N (expected decompositions per target)
====================================================================================================
  m=2 l*=43.3333: lambda = 2^-45.3333; 1/lambda vs 1/P(decomposable) differ by factor 1.0 (relative error ~lambda/2 = 2^-46.333)
  m=3 l*=33.0: lambda = 2^-34.585; 1/lambda vs 1/P(decomposable) differ by factor 1.0 (relative error ~lambda/2 = 2^-35.585)
  m=4 l*=26.834: lambda = 2^-28.249; 1/lambda vs 1/P(decomposable) differ by factor 1.0 (relative error ~lambda/2 = 2^-29.249)
  m=5 l*=22.7642: lambda = 2^-24.0861; 1/lambda vs 1/P(decomposable) differ by factor 1.0 (relative error ~lambda/2 = 2^-25.086)
  m=6 l*=19.8898: lambda = 2^-21.1529; 1/lambda vs 1/P(decomposable) differ by factor 1.0000002 (relative error ~lambda/2 = 2^-22.153)
  m=8 l*=16.123: lambda = 2^-17.3156; 1/lambda vs 1/P(decomposable) differ by factor 1.0000031 (relative error ~lambda/2 = 2^-18.316)
  -> at every floor-optimal l the m-subset sums cover a 2^-20 .. 2^-1 fraction of the group, so the Poisson correction is negligible;
     the UNIFORM-SPREAD heuristic itself (sums equidistributed over cosets / the group) is measured by the producer at n <= 19 only (note section 1) and is a transfer assumption at n = 131.

====================================================================================================
J1(c) FROBENIUS-STABLE-SET VARIANT m*2^n/n: arithmetic facts at n = 131
====================================================================================================
  ord_131(2) = 130  -> 2-cyclotomic cosets mod 131 have sizes 1 and 130; invariant F_2-subspace dims: 0, 1, 130, 131
  131 prime and every x not in F_2 has Frobenius orbit of size exactly 131 (orbit size divides 131): orbit unions of k orbits have 131k abscissae for any k -> 'free' as SETS
  Frobenius eigenvalue on <G> exists with order 131 iff 131 | r-1: (r-1) mod 131 = 0
  collapsed product: (2^l/n) * N/C(2^l,m) * C(2^l,m-1) = m N /n  -> log2(m*2^131/131) at m=3: 125.5515
  orbit-collapsed (relations /n, LA /n^2) free-oracle floor m=2: l*=45.6778 floor=2^79.8737  (producer no-collapse floor 2^89.2516)
  orbit-collapsed (relations /n, LA /n^2) free-oracle floor m=3: l*=34.7584 floor=2^58.0348  (producer no-collapse floor 2^68.585)
  orbit-collapsed (relations /n, LA /n^2) free-oracle floor m=4: l*=28.2407 floor=2^45.1515  (producer no-collapse floor 2^56.4049)
  orbit-collapsed (relations /n, LA /n^2) free-oracle floor m=5: l*=23.9364 floor=2^36.7128  (producer no-collapse floor 2^48.4352)
  orbit-collapsed (relations /n, LA /n^2) free-oracle floor m=6: l*=20.8946 floor=2^30.7928  (producer no-collapse floor 2^42.8501)
  orbit-collapsed (relations /n, LA /n^2) free-oracle floor m=8: l*=16.9045 floor=2^23.1047  (producer no-collapse floor 2^35.6086)
  -> the collapse moves the m=3 floor by about log2(131)*2/(m+1) ~ 3.5 bits (rel/LA collapse) but m=3 stays above rho; the FINDING's floor table is the NO-collapse table and says so ('2^l/n for a Frobenius-stable set').

====================================================================================================
J2 ORACLE-BUDGET COLUMN, REQUIRED SPEED-UP, GAP -- three DIFFERENT quantities, pinned
====================================================================================================
  D1 gap      G(m) = rho / floor(l*)                      : headroom on the TOTAL free-oracle cost
  D2 budget   B(m) = rho / T(l*)   (T = targets term alone): per-target oracle cost at which targets x oracle = rho at the FLOOR-OPTIMAL l, LA ignored
  D2' exact   B'(m) = (rho - L(l*)) / T(l*)                : same with the LA term charged
  D3 re-opt   W(m) : per-target oracle cost 2^w at which min over l of [2^w T(l) + L(l)] = rho  (l re-optimised for the oracle)
  D4 speed-up S(m) = rho / (m N)  (= B(m)/C(2^l*, m-1))     : required speed-up over exhaustive search of the C(|F|,m-1) sub-tuples
